# PART 4 — CALCULATION ENGINE SPECIFICATION

## 9. Date and Absence Calculation Rules

This is the most important component of the entire application.

All calculations must be deterministic, reproducible and independently testable.

### 9.1 Date Handling

- Use calendar dates, not local timestamps.
- Handle leap years.
- Handle month boundaries.
- Handle year boundaries.
- Avoid timezone-related date shifts.
- Validate all date inputs.

### 9.2 Naturalisation Counting Method

For the standard 5-year naturalisation route:

Count only complete days spent outside the UK.

Formula:

```text
Naturalisation absence days =
Return date - Departure date - 1
```

Example:

Departure: 1 June  
Return: 10 June

Counted absence: 8 days.

The departure date and return date are excluded.

A same-day departure and return results in zero whole days of absence.

### 9.3 ILR Continuous Residence Calculation

The standard BN(O) settlement route normally requires:

- Five years of qualifying continuous residence.
- No more than 180 days outside the UK in any applicable 12-month period.

The calculation engine must:

1. Evaluate every relevant calendar date within the qualifying period.
2. Construct the applicable 12-calendar-month windows.
3. Calculate the absence-day total for each window.
4. Identify the maximum absence count.
5. Return the relevant window start and end dates.
6. Compare the result against the normal 180-day threshold.

Do not simply calculate one total per trip.

Do not assume that checking only departure and return dates is sufficient.

**Important implementation constraint:** The exact treatment of travel boundary dates for the ILR rule must be verified against the applicable Home Office guidance before the engine is labelled officially compliant. Keep this calculation method configurable and document the chosen interpretation.

Do not silently reuse the Naturalisation whole-day counting method for ILR.

### 9.4 Naturalisation Five-Year Calculation

Use the standard five-year naturalisation qualifying period.

Normal absence threshold:

450 days.

The qualifying period must be calculated using calendar-year arithmetic, not a fixed 1,825 or 1,826-day assumption.

The application must also remind users that they generally need to have been physically present in the UK on the first day of the qualifying period.

### 9.5 Naturalisation Final 12-Month Calculation

Normal absence threshold:

90 days.

Calculate the relevant 12-calendar-month period before the proposed application date.

Use the same whole-day counting methodology as the overall naturalisation calculation.

### 9.6 ILR Eligibility Date

The normal earliest application date is 28 days before completing five years of qualifying residence.

Conceptually:

```text
Five-year anniversary =
Qualifying period start date + 5 calendar years

Earliest application date =
Five-year anniversary - 28 days
```

This is an estimate subject to the applicable qualifying-period rules and user-confirmed dates.

Do not use a fixed 1,826-day calculation.

### 9.7 Naturalisation Eligibility Date

For the standard route, a person generally needs to have held ILR for at least 12 months before applying.

Conceptually:

```text
Earliest naturalisation date =
Actual ILR grant date + 12 calendar months
```

This must use calendar-month arithmetic, not a fixed 365-day addition.

Naturalisation also requires a separate five-year qualifying-period assessment.

Do not automatically assume that meeting the date and absence thresholds means the user qualifies for citizenship.

